    "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point",
    "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=2{x}^{2}+x$ at the point $(1,3).$",
    "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+x)=4x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1)=4(1)+1=5\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=5$ and the coordinates of our point $(1,3),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(3) & =5(x-1) \\\\ y & =5x-5+3 \\\\ y & =5x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
